00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:02.220
GABRIELA D'AVILA FERRARA:
With almost 500 sessions

00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:05.280
at Next 2019, it's
understandable

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:07.390
that you couldn't
get to them all.

00:00:07.390 --> 00:00:10.530
So if you didn't see Tools
for Migrating your Databases

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:13.180
to Google Cloud,
then stay tuned,

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:14.730
because here's the rewind.

00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:16.218
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:19.690 --> 00:00:22.090
In this session, learn
about the options

00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:26.020
to migrate your current workload
to Google Cloud Platform

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:28.540
and ways that you can
minimize the disruption

00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:31.180
that a migration can cause.

00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:35.050
You will learn about
three steps for migration.

00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:39.010
First is the assessment
to figure out what to move

00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:42.670
and if it is ready to be
moved, as well to learn

00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:45.940
about the new path
to the cloud and how

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:49.750
the support for that
workload would be.

00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:52.850
Second is the migration itself.

00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:56.500
If this will be a simple
rehost or complete rewrite--

00:00:56.500 --> 00:00:58.630
it will depend on your needs.

00:00:58.630 --> 00:01:01.450
And third is the tuning
and optimization part.

00:01:01.450 --> 00:01:05.050
How do I get better,
faster, and cheaper?

00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:07.150
To help with all
of those steps, we

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:11.320
have migration partners, which
we showcased in this session,

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:14.590
to help you achieve either
your homogeneous migration--

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:21.340
for instance, MySQL to Cloud
SQL or HBase to Cloud Bigtable,

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:23.920
or if it is a
heterogeneous migration,

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:27.910
like Oracle to
Cloud SQL Postgres.

00:01:27.910 --> 00:01:31.210
This session also shows how
a migration to Cloud SQL

00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:33.310
can be done by yourself.

00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:36.670
And this could happen
mainly in two ways--

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:41.200
a simple approach or a
minimum downtime approach.

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:43.300
If you can afford
downtime and this

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:47.440
won't affect your business, you
can go through the simple route

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:50.140
by doing an offline
migration where

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:53.230
you get all your
data into a dump file

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:56.110
and import it to a new
Cloud SQL instance.

00:01:56.110 --> 00:01:58.450
Once this phase is
completed, then you

00:01:58.450 --> 00:02:00.730
can put all systems
online again.

00:02:00.730 --> 00:02:04.460
If your application cannot
take a significant downtime,

00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:07.960
then another solution that
we have is using Cloud SQL

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:12.760
as an external replica
to your primary server.

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:15.010
The way this works
is by spinning up

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:18.700
a new instance of Cloud
SQL, using the Migrate Data

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:22.450
option in reference to
your primary server.

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:25.630
That way, replication
will start and catch up.

00:02:25.630 --> 00:02:29.200
Meaning that every change you
made in your previous server

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:34.290
will be applied to the new
one on Google Cloud Platform.

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:36.760
Once replication
gets up, it is time

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:40.180
to do the work of stopping
replication, promoting

00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:43.960
your replica to primary, and
making sure your application is

00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:46.630
aware of the new
server to be able to do

00:02:46.630 --> 00:02:50.320
reads and writes on the
new Cloud SQL instance.

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:52.630
The effective time
taken on this model

00:02:52.630 --> 00:02:56.530
will depend on a few variables
that this session explains,

00:02:56.530 --> 00:02:58.870
such as DNS propagation.

00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:00.760
